{%- set pkg = cookiecutter.__package_name -%}
## Calling Rust from Python

This workspace is a Python extension module: `crates/py` compiles to a `cdylib` named `_core`,
which `python/{{pkg}}/` imports and a wheel ships.

```sh
mise run develop   # build the module into .venv, in place
mise run wheel     # release wheel into dist/
```

The files that carry the interop:

- `crates/py/` - the `#[pymodule]`; an abi3 `cdylib` named `_core`, not published to crates.io
- `python/{{pkg}}/` - `__init__.py` re-exporting the public API, `_core.pyi` typing the compiled
  module, and `py.typed`
- `tests/` - pytest over the boundary, separate from `crates/py`'s own `#[test]`s
- `scripts/` - toolchain helpers (MSRV check, toolchain bump); not interop, but pytest covers
  it alongside `tests/`
- `pyproject.toml` - a maturin `[build-system]`, so this is a Python distribution. `package = true`,
  and the version is `dynamic`, read from `Cargo.toml`

Rules that matter when editing it:

- `crates/core` must stay free of pyo3. Anything Python needs from it is a trait, implemented in
  `crates/py`. Put logic in `core`, not in the interop layer.
- pyo3's `extension-module` is enabled by maturin alone, through `[tool.maturin] features` in
  `pyproject.toml`. Never make it a default feature of `crates/py`: it omits the libpython symbols
  the host interpreter supplies at import, so every test binary would need
  `--no-default-features`, which cargo applies to every crate in the workspace.
- Return core errors as `PyErr` through a conversion function, as `score_error` maps
  `NegativeInput` to `ScoreError`; a `From` impl is barred, since both types are foreign to
  `crates/py`. A panic crossing the boundary aborts the interpreter.
- Hand the GIL back with `py.detach` around real Rust work, so other Python threads run and
  `rayon` inside the detached block pays off. Still cross once per batch rather than once per
  element.
- Keep `_core.pyi` in step with the `#[pymodule]` surface. `tests/test_stubs.py` checks the names
  agree; the signatures are unchecked.
- Callers import `{{pkg}}`, never `{{pkg}}._core`. The `__init__.py` re-export is what lets the
  Rust side move.
- `mise run py-test` depends on `develop`, because pytest cannot import a module that has not been
  built. After changing Rust, rebuild before testing from Python.
{%- if cookiecutter.crate_type != 'lib' %}
- `crates/cli` is pure Rust over the same core, with no pyo3 and no `python` feature. It is a
  separate consumer of `crates/core`, not a consumer of the extension module.
{%- endif %}
